window.docContents[207].resolve({"/#example-cat-streams":{"contents":"Now that the basic skeleton of the program has been built, it's time to actually enter the code.\nA proper implementation of cat can be used with infinite IO streams, such as /dev/random, which means that it can't read its input into memory before outputting it.\nFurthermore, it should not work one character at a time, as this leads to frustratingly slow performance.\nInstead, it's better to read contiguous blocks of data all at once, directing the data to the standard output one block at a time.\n\nThe first step is to decide how big of a block to read.\nFor the sake of simplicity, this implementation uses a conservative 20 kilobyte block.\n is analogous to size_t in C—it's an unsigned integer type that is big enough to represent all valid array sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Worked Example:  cat","header":"2.4.2. Concatenating Streams","id":"/#example-cat-streams"},"/#props-proofs-indexing":{"contents":"Like many languages, Lean uses square brackets for indexing into arrays and lists.\nFor instance, if  is defined as follows:\n\n\n\nthen the individual components can be extracted:\n\n\n\nHowever, attempting to extract the fourth element results in a compile-time error, rather than a run-time error:\n\n\n\n\n\nThis error message is saying Lean tried to automatically mathematically prove that  (i.e. ), which would mean that the lookup was safe, but that it could not do so.\nOut-of-bounds errors are a common class of bugs, and Lean uses its dual nature as a programming language and a theorem prover to rule out as many as possible.\n\nUnderstanding how this works requires an understanding of three key ideas: propositions, proofs, and tactics.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean","header":"Interlude: Propositions, Proofs, and Indexing","id":"/#props-proofs-indexing"}});